From cea899515d24a02451f8649b4a0299146e4b8bfb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arttttt <artem-bambalov@yandex.ru>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 00:32:22 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] jsoncpp: SecureAllocator takes a plain pointer, as in 1.9.5

C++20 deprecates volatile-qualified parameters, and deallocate() took
one: the header no longer builds with -std=c++20 -Werror, which is how
the camera HAL builds. jsoncpp 1.9.5 dropped the qualifier and zeroes
the block with memset_s instead -- the same change backported here,
hunk for hunk, from AOSP's android13-release, where external/jsoncpp
is 1.9.5.

The allocator is only instantiated with JSONCPP_USING_SECURE_MEMORY,
which Android leaves off, so bionic having no memset_s does not matter;
that is why 1.9.5 builds on Android 13 as it is.

Drop this patch when moving to Android 13: its jsoncpp has it.
---
 include/json/allocator.h | 7 +++----
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/json/allocator.h b/include/json/allocator.h
index 0f5c224..95ef8a5 100644
--- a/include/json/allocator.h
+++ b/include/json/allocator.h
@@ -35,11 +35,10 @@ public:
    * Release memory which was allocated for N items at pointer P.
    *
    * The memory block is filled with zeroes before being released.
-   * The pointer argument is tagged as "volatile" to prevent the
-   * compiler optimizing out this critical step.
    */
-  void deallocate(volatile pointer p, size_type n) {
-    std::memset(p, 0, n * sizeof(T));
+  void deallocate(pointer p, size_type n) {
+    // memset_s is used because memset may be optimized away by the compiler
+    memset_s(p, n * sizeof(T), 0, n * sizeof(T));
     // free using "global operator delete"
     ::operator delete(p);
   }
